>
    <h2>Call first, then come in</h2>
    <p>Phoning ahead means a nurse can tell you what to do on the way, a room is ready when you arrive and a vet knows what to expect. If your pet needs to be seen immediately, we will say so and see you straight away.</p>
    <h2>What we can treat on the day</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Vomiting, diarrhoea and pets who will not eat.</li>
        <li>Limping, cuts, torn nails and bite wounds.</li>
        <li>Itchy, sore or infected ears, eyes and skin.</li>
        <li>Urinary problems: straining, blood, or going more often.</li>
        <li>Swallowed things that might not pass: we have X-ray and ultrasound here.</li>
        <li>Allergic reactions, bee stings and swollen faces.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout">A male cat straining in the litter tray and producing nothing is an emergency. Call us, or Lantern Hill after hours, straight away.</p>
    <h2>When it is an emergency</h2>
    <p>Difficulty breathing, collapse, a seizure that does not stop, a swollen hard belly in a dog, heavy bleeding or a known poison need immediate care. Do not wait for an appointment: call as you set off. Our <a href="/emergency/">emergency page</a> has the full list and what to do on the way.</p>
</ServicePage>
